POST http://localhost:3001/api/v1 HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3ExDEeSnyvE&t=6496s"
}
###
#----------------------------------------
# //Redis Container shell
# docker exec -it url_shortner-redis-1 redis-cli
# //now issuing raw Redis commands directly, bypassing your Go app entirely, to see the actual stored data
# //Inside that prompt, run:
# SELECT 0
# //Redis has 16 numbered databases (0–15) by default, all within the same server — 
# //your app uses DB 0 for the URL cache (CreateClient(0)) and DB 1 for rate limiting (CreateClient(1)). 
# //SELECT 0 switches your current session to the same DB your Cache is writing to.
# //Then:
# GET jekooh
# //Expected output — the exact long URL, quoted:
# "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3ExDEeSnyvE&t=5596s"
# //This proves the Set call in ShortenURL actually persisted the data, independent of whether ResolveURL can retrieve it — 
# //isolating "is it stored" from "is it retrieved" as two separate facts.
# //While you're in here, also check:
# TTL jekooh
# //Should return a positive number of seconds (close to 24 * 3600 = 86400, counting down) — confirms the 24-hour expiry was actually applied.
# //Exit the Redis shell when done:
# exit
